# The weather words on a forecast chart.
lesson language-weather v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=7 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "[[phrase.what-is-the-weather-like]]"
  goal "Say what the weather is like in [[language.name]], from a picture or a forecast."

  look {
    scene 19x11 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        weatherweek chart days=["[[time.monday]]", "[[time.tuesday]]", "[[time.wednesday]]", "[[time.thursday]]"] skies=["sun", "rain", "wind", "snow"] temps=["28 °C", "18 °C", "20 °C", "1 °C"]
      }
    }
    say """
      To ask about the weather, say [[phrase.what-is-the-weather-like]] On Monday
      [[weather.it-is-sunny]], it is sunny. On Tuesday [[weather.it-is-raining]], it rains. On
      Wednesday [[weather.it-is-windy]], it is windy. On Thursday [[weather.it-is-snowing]], it
      snows, and [[weather.it-is-cold]], it is cold. When it is hot, [[weather.it-is-hot]].
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.weather-name k=4
    }
    show language.weather-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.weather-meaning k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show language.weather-write k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.weather-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.weather-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.weather-write k=5 easy-k=2 hard-k=6
    show language.weather-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.weather-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.weather-write k=4
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-weather-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.weather-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[phrase.what-is-the-weather-like]] Answer with [[weather.it-is-sunny]], [[weather.it-is-raining]], [[weather.it-is-snowing]], [[weather.it-is-windy]], [[weather.it-is-hot]] or [[weather.it-is-cold]]."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is mixing sunny with hot: a sunny day can be cold, and the chart
    gives the temperature under the picture where it matters. The forecast chart draws each sky as a
    shape, not a colour, so it reads the same in print. The listening question is a dictation: read
    the sentence aloud from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or let the device play it. It
    is marked word by word, and a missing accent counts there, while the words typed on their own in
    this lesson are taken without their accents. If the child is stuck, read the chart aloud in
    English, then find each phrase in the language. Off the page, say the weather in the language at
    breakfast each day for a week and keep a chart. It meets England's key stage 2 languages
    programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "describe people, places, things and actions
    orally and in writing"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign languages: "Can listen to and understand
    concrete information regarding familiar and simple matters in daily life if spoken to slowly and
    clearly"; and CEFR A1, "Can understand and use familiar everyday expressions and very basic
    phrases". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[weather.it-is-cloudy]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[weather.it-is-sunny]] and [[weather.it-is-raining]] and the words to write are [[weather.sun]] and [[weather.it-is-snowing]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[weather.there-is-a-storm]] and [[weather.it-is-hot]] and the words to write are [[weather.heat]], [[weather.cold]] and [[weather.cloudy]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
